<!--
  ===================================================================
   LINEAR ELASTICITY BENCHMARK 4 — PLATE WITH A HOLE (Kirsch)
  ===================================================================

   PROBLEM DEFINITION
     A large plate with a central circular hole of radius R, under
     remote uniaxial tension S in the x-direction. By symmetry only
     one quarter is modelled (hole centred at the origin corner),
     meshed as a structured O-grid from the hole edge (r=R) out to
     the straight outer square boundary [0,W]x[0,W]. One hex layer
     in z (plane-stress-like slab).
       R = 0.5, W = 5.0 (quarter half-width), thickness = 0.1
       E = 1000.0, nu = 0.3
       S = 1.0  (remote tension, +x)
       O-grid = 10 radial x 12 angular

   BOUNDARY CONDITIONS
     x = 0 cut (y-axis) : symmetry, u_x = 0
     y = 0 cut (x-axis) : symmetry, u_y = 0
     x = W edge         : Neumann tension  tx = S  (marker 10)
     z = 0 layer        : u_z = 0  (thin-slab out-of-plane stabilisation)
     hole edge r=R      : traction-free

   EXACT / REFERENCE SOLUTION  (Kirsch, 1898)
     For an INFINITE plate, the hoop stress on the hole boundary is
       sigma_theta(R, theta) = S (1 - 2 cos 2theta)
     giving the maximum at theta = 90deg (top of the hole, perpendicular
     to the load):
       STRESS CONCENTRATION FACTOR Kt = 3
       sigma_theta_max = 3 S = 3
     and the minimum at theta = 0 (sides, along the load): -S.
     (W/R finite here, so expect Kt slightly below 3; it approaches 3
      as W/R grows. Compare sig_xx at the hole top node vs 3S.)
  -->
